A||||3||FieroMike||Air Intake
Z||000000||FieroMike||04-26-99||10:57 AM||||Here is my experience with changing the air-intake:<p>I really wanted to un-restrict the air-flow because I had read somewhere that the air went through a hole the size of a flat-head screwdriver.  I took off the grill and the wheel well cover and took a look.  I saw the hole that they were talking about, however the air goes up and over in a 2 1'2 inch oblong opening.  It's like a big air filter box.  Well, even though that was a lot better I still wanted to improve it bcz it was still restricting flow.  So I removed the entire box (2 lbs worth) the bracket and all. This leaves the rubber grommet on the intake on the side of the car and the grommet hanging off the bottom of the air filter cylinder. I took some vent tubing (A rigid flexible tubing for the vents in your ceiling, or on the floor) for $4.00 and brought it straight across over.  This shortens the travel by about 1/2 a foot and has no restriction.  However don't go driving through puddles of water!!  I then replaced the air filter with a K&N Filter(charger).  The difference was HUGE!!  When I started the car I thought I heard a huge air-leak and popped the deck lid to see what I didn't connect, but it was the vent on the side of the car SUCKING AIR.  I was impressed.  I felt the difference so I took it to my friend that works at BIG-O who has a dyno.  Initially my car was 126 HP when I bought it.  Plugs, Wires, Cap, Rotor, All Filters, PCV Valve, FI cleaner, and Synthetic oil brought the car back to life at 139 HP.  With this MOD I gained about 5 HP @ 4700 RPM and 3HP at 2900 RPM. Who knows how much if the car was actually moving, forcing air in.<br>I also added a MSD Coil and gained 2 HP at 5200 RPM (Peak HP)  SO I am running a good 144-146 HP. Not bad.  Sorry so Long!! Take Care!
Z||000001||theogre||04-26-99||07:42 PM||||when ever you dyno a car also record the torq numbers. HP changes only tell a part of the picture. torq changes tell the rest. <p>for example, if you gained HP, but lost torq, the car will now have a worse "hole shot" than it did before the change. It may go faster once it gets rolling, but it will take longer to get rolling, so that the total percieved performance increase in HP may actually be a decrease in the real total.
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||04-26-99||08:26 PM||||Mike,                                       <br>Where is the MAT sensor located now?? Any check engine light ?<p>Does the rigid flex tube create any turbulence?
Z||000003||FieroMike||04-28-99||01:00 PM||||Ogre:<br>Sorry about that, The Torque changed very little, about 1lb-ft - 2lb between 2800 - 3200 and then it very slowly changed to about 3lb-ft by the time it came to 5200 rpm, I noticed it moved the power hump at 3500 down to about 3300-3400.  Not huge but better than before!  I guess it didn't change very much as the RPM's went up because no air was being forced into the intake, I would be interested to see soe equiptment that forced air in as the speed of the car increased.<p>DXR_DAD,<br>I retained the stock position, the air filter canister is still there, I only took out what was below the canister to the outside of the car.  The can is probably still more restrictive that just running a tube with a K&N at the end, but like you said I would not want to drill a hole in the tube and tie in the MAT sensor and the other tube sensor on the bottom.  Too much work for right now.  This mod should take you 15 minutes at the most. Later Guys! (And ladies) :-)<p>P.s. Ogre - The msd ignition did nothing for the Torque.
